# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# omniroute-coder — production environment template
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Copy this to .env.production, fill in the values, and never commit it.
# (.gitignore already excludes `.env*`; docker-compose.yml loads .env.production
# via env_file, so values are not visible to `docker inspect`.)
#
#   cp .env.production.example .env.production
#
# Everything below is a variable the code actually reads — this list was taken
# from the source, not written from memory. Blank means "feature off", and the
# app degrades rather than crashing: with no email provider the sign-in dialog
# says so, with no gateway each user connects their own.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# READ THIS FIRST — what will stop the server from starting
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The boot check in src/lib/productionGuard.ts refuses to start a production
# server in FOUR configurations. The first two leave a working app that looks
# completely normal while having no authentication at all:
#
#   AUTH_DEV_SHOW_OTP=true    returns the sign-in code in the API response, so
#                             anyone can sign in as anyone. ONLY for localhost.
#   UPI_AUTO_APPROVE=true     marks every payment order as paid on creation, so
#                             typing twelve digits grants PRO for free.
#
# The next two are about the secret that signs sessions and encrypts stored
# provider keys:
#
#   AUTH_SECRET unset         the app would generate one and store it in the
#                             same database it protects.
#   AUTH_SECRET or            set but under 16 characters, or with very few
#   CREDENTIALS_SECRET weak   distinct characters — i.e. typed by a person
#                             rather than generated.
#
# All four are listed below in their safe state. The admin panel's auto-approve
# toggle writes to the database and takes precedence over the env var, so check
# the panel too before deploying.
#
# There used to be a fifth: OMNIROUTE_ENABLE_FILE_TOOLS=true was fatal, because
# the file tools read and wrote the SERVER's own disk and every signed-in user
# shared it. They no longer do — every operation is routed to the calling user's
# own VS Code, and a production build refuses to fall back to this machine's
# filesystem — so enabling them is now the intended configuration. See §9.
#
# To override deliberately: OMNIROUTE_ALLOW_UNSAFE_CONFIG=true downgrades the
# refusal to a warning. Do not set this to get past a first deploy.
# ===========================================================================


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Core secrets — set these, they are the ones that matter
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Signs session cookies and (as a fallback) encrypts stored provider keys.
# Generate:  openssl rand -hex 32
# If left blank the app generates one and stores it in the database, which works
# as long as the database is on a persistent volume. It is set explicitly here
# so that losing the volume does not silently sign everyone out — and, more
# importantly, so provider credentials stay decryptable.
AUTH_SECRET=

# Encrypts the provider API keys users save (AES-256-GCM). Defaults to
# AUTH_SECRET when blank. Set it separately if you ever want to rotate sessions
# without making every stored credential unreadable — but note that changing
# this value makes existing stored credentials undecryptable, and users have to
# re-enter their keys.
CREDENTIALS_SECRET=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Who gets admin
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Bootstrap allowlist, checked at sign-in. When one of these addresses or phone
# numbers signs in, the account is promoted to admin. This is the only way the
# first admin comes into existence — there is no admin password any more.
#
# Comma-separated. ADMIN_EMAIL is still honoured as a single-address alias for
# older setups, but prefer ADMIN_EMAILS.
ADMIN_EMAILS=
ADMIN_EMAIL=
ADMIN_PHONES=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Email — this is what actually delivers sign-in codes
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# WHICH ONE DO I PICK? The honest answer depends on one question: do you own a
# domain yet?
#
#   NO DOMAIN  ->  Gmail App Password (see below). It delivers to ANY address,
#                  needs nothing but a Google account, and is free. This is the
#                  right choice for a beta whose testers you are recruiting over
#                  WhatsApp and LinkedIn, because their addresses are arbitrary
#                  and you cannot add them to anything in advance.
#
#   DOMAIN     ->  Resend with that domain verified. Better deliverability, a
#                  sender address that looks like your product, and it scales on
#                  a paid plan without any code change.
#
# What you must NOT do is the middle path that looks easiest: set RESEND_API_KEY
# and leave MAIL_FROM at onboarding@resend.dev. That combination delivers to YOU
# and to nobody else — every other tester's send comes back HTTP 422 and they
# simply cannot sign in. It is not a visible failure on your own account, which
# is what makes it worth this much text. AUTH_BETA_TESTERS in §5 exists as the
# escape hatch for exactly this situation; read what it costs before using it.
#
# Switching later is an env change and a restart. emailProvider() in
# src/lib/otpDelivery.ts picks the first configured provider in the order
# Resend -> SMTP -> Gmail, so filling in RESEND_API_KEY later takes precedence
# over the Gmail values below without you having to clear them.

# Resend delivers over HTTPS, which is why it is checked first: it works even on
# a host that blocks outbound SMTP entirely. Free tier covers 100/day and
# 3,000/month. Get a key at https://resend.com/api-keys
#
# Until you verify your own domain with Resend, MAIL_FROM must be
# onboarding@resend.dev, and Resend will only deliver to the address that owns
# the account. Verify a domain to send to anyone.
RESEND_API_KEY=

# Sender address on the code email. Defaults to onboarding@resend.dev.
# Once a domain is verified: "OmniRoute <no-reply@yourdomain.com>"
MAIL_FROM=

# --- SMTP (any provider, including SES/Postmark/Mailgun) ---------------------
# Used when RESEND_API_KEY is empty, and takes precedence over the Gmail
# shorthand below. Reach for this if you already run mail somewhere.
SMTP_HOST=
SMTP_PORT=
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASS=

# --- Gmail — the no-domain path, and the right one for a first beta ----------
# Setup, once, about five minutes:
#   1. Turn on 2-Step Verification: https://myaccount.google.com/security
#      (App Passwords do not exist as an option until you do.)
#   2. Create an App Password: https://myaccount.google.com/apppasswords
#      Name it anything. You get 16 characters, shown once.
#   3. GMAIL_USER = the full address. GMAIL_APP_PASSWORD = those 16 characters,
#      spaces removed. Your normal account password will NOT work — Google
#      rejects it outright, which reads as a credentials error in the log.
#
# On AWS EC2 specifically this works out of the box. AWS throttles outbound
# port 25 by default and does not restrict 465 or 587, and nodemailer uses 587
# here — so the usual "my host blocks SMTP" problem does not apply to the target
# in AWS_DEPLOYMENT_CHECKLIST.md. The default security group allows all egress;
# if you have narrowed yours, 587 outbound has to be open.
#
# Limits worth knowing before you rely on it: consumer Gmail caps around 500
# recipients/day, the sender address is visibly a personal Gmail rather than
# your product, and Google can challenge the account if traffic looks unusual.
# For a beta of tens of people that is all fine. It is not where you want to be
# at launch — move to Resend on a verified domain when you have a domain.
GMAIL_USER=
GMAIL_APP_PASSWORD=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. SMS — read the DLT note before spending anything
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A2P SMS to Indian numbers requires DLT registration with the operator:
# entity + sender ID + template registration. This is a regulatory filing, not a
# code change. Without it, correctly-configured Twilio credentials are rejected
# upstream — messages are filtered before delivery.
#
# With no SMS provider configured, phone sign-in reports that it is unavailable
# and points the user at email. That is the intended behaviour, not an error.
#
# E.164 country code prepended to locally-typed numbers, digits only.
OTP_SMS_COUNTRY_CODE=91

TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID=
TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=
TWILIO_PHONE_NUMBER=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. One-time codes and sessions
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ⚠ DANGER on a public deployment — see the header. Returning the code in the
# API response means anyone who can reach the server can sign in as anyone.
# Leave false or unset in production. The boot check refuses to start if true.
AUTH_DEV_SHOW_OTP=false

# A narrow, named exception to the line above — for the gap between "the server
# is live" and "the sending domain is verified".
#
# The usual first deployment sends through Resend's shared test address,
# onboarding@resend.dev, which is only permitted to deliver to the inbox that
# owns the Resend account. Everybody else's sign-in attempt comes back HTTP 422
# and they simply cannot get in. Listing an account here lets THAT account, and
# only that account, see its own code in the login dialog when a configured
# provider genuinely fails.
#
# It is not AUTH_DEV_SHOW_OTP with extra steps. Four conditions all have to
# hold: the account is named here, a provider was configured, it was tried, and
# it failed for a reason other than rate-limiting. That last one is not
# arbitrary — burning the provider's send quota until it answers 429 is the only
# way an outsider could force a failure on demand, so a rate-limit failure never
# reveals anything.
#
# The honest residual risk: anyone who CAN break your mail provider can read a
# listed account's code, and at that point they are that account. Clear this
# once real delivery works. The boot check prints the list on every start so it
# cannot be left on and forgotten.
#
# Comma-separated emails and/or 10-digit phone numbers. Empty = feature off.
AUTH_BETA_TESTERS=

OTP_EXPIRY_MINUTES=10
SESSION_EXPIRY_DAYS=30


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. Payments (UPI) — manual reconciliation by design
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The UPI ID and price are configured in the admin panel, not here: they are the
# merchant account that receives money, so they are a single global setting
# rather than something each user brings.
#
# ⚠ Set false. When true, every order is marked paid the instant it is created
# and PRO is free to anyone. The admin panel toggle overrides this value.
UPI_AUTO_APPROVE=false

# How long a payment order stays valid before the amount is released. The
# per-order paise suffix (see src/lib/upi.ts) is what makes each transfer
# uniquely matchable; unmatched orders expire rather than tying up amounts
# forever.
UPI_ORDER_TTL_MINUTES=30


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 7. AI gateway
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Each signed-in user stores their OWN gateway URL and API key (encrypted, in
# the database) from the settings panel. These two variables are only a shared
# FALLBACK for users who have not connected their own.
#
# Do NOT point this at a localhost gateway on a hosted server: localhost there
# means the server itself, so nothing reaches the OmniRoute instance running on
# your PC. The boot check warns about exactly this.
OMNIROUTE_BASE_URL=
OMNIROUTE_API_KEY=

# May a user who has connected nothing borrow the two values above?
#
# Unset means false in production and true in development, which is the safe
# default: on a public server the shared fallback hands YOUR gateway key to
# every stranger who signs up, and your quota pays for their chatting.
#
# Set true ONLY if this deployment is for you and people you trust, and you
# intend to fund everyone's usage from one key. Leave it unset/false for a
# public sign-up, and each user adds their own gateway in Settings.
OMNIROUTE_ALLOW_SHARED_GATEWAY=

# Optional, admin-only. When set, the quota panel shows this OpenRouter
# balance — but only to an admin, because the key belongs to the deployment
# rather than to whoever is signed in (see src/app/api/credits/route.ts).
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=

# Set true ONLY if allowing users to point at private/loopback addresses is
# intended. The SSRF guard blocks private targets by default, which is what
# stops a user using the server as a proxy into the host's own network.
OMNIROUTE_ALLOW_PRIVATE_GATEWAY=false

# Model plumbing, all optional — defaults live in src/lib/omniroute.ts.
# Lists are comma-separated.
OMNIROUTE_ALLOWED_PROVIDERS=
OMNIROUTE_COMBO_PREFIXES=
OMNIROUTE_PROVIDER_PRIORITY=
OMNIROUTE_EXTRA_MODELS=
OMNIROUTE_ACCOUNT_RETRIES=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 8. Web search and agent behaviour
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional. Without it the built-in web search tool reports that it is not
# configured; chatting is unaffected. https://tavily.com
TAVILY_API_KEY=

# Ceiling on tool steps in a single chat turn. Higher means longer agent runs
# and more tokens spent per message.
OMNIROUTE_MAX_STEPS=

# Deep Cowork round budget. The pipeline stops when it plateaus, so these are
# bounds rather than targets. Raising the ceiling raises worst-case cost.
OMNIROUTE_DEEP_MAX_ITERATIONS=
OMNIROUTE_DEEP_MAX_CEILING=
OMNIROUTE_DEEP_EXTEND=
OMNIROUTE_DEEP_STALL_TOLERANCE=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 9. File tools — now safe in production, because they no longer touch this box
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This section used to say the opposite, and the change is worth understanding
# rather than taking on trust.
#
# Cowork and Deep Cowork used to read and write files on the machine running
# this server, with no per-user isolation: every signed-in user saw the same
# workspace, so any user could read any file the process could reach — including
# .env and the database holding everyone's encrypted provider keys. Enabling
# them in production was fatal at boot, and rightly so.
#
# They now work through the VS Code extension. Every read, write, replace and
# listing is routed to the editor belonging to the account that asked, and in a
# production build there is NO fallback to this machine's filesystem: if that
# user's editor is not connected, the operation fails and says so. Nothing on
# this server is reachable through them, which is why the fatal is gone.
#
# Leave this blank to get the sensible default: on when the editor bridge is on
# (§11), off when it is not. Set it explicitly only to override that.
OMNIROUTE_ENABLE_FILE_TOOLS=

# Escape hatch that permits paths outside the workspace root. Leave unset.
# NOTE: this one is read as `=== "1"` (src/lib/vscodeBridge.ts), so the ONLY
# value that enables it is the literal 1. "true" does not work here, unlike the
# other switches in this file. Anything else — including blank — keeps it off.
#
# It is also inert in production: "allow the model outside the workspace root"
# is not a statement this process is in a position to make about a folder on
# somebody else's laptop, so the guard ignores it there.
OMNIROUTE_ALLOW_OUTSIDE_ROOT=

# IGNORED in production. Leave it blank.
#
# It pins the agent to a directory on THIS machine, which is meaningful on a
# laptop and meaningless once the workspace is whatever folder the connected
# user has open in their own editor. The boot check warns if it is set, because
# an operator who sets it expects something to happen and nothing does.
OMNIROUTE_WORKSPACE_ROOT=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 10. Deployment plumbing
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Where the SQLite file lives. In the container this is /data/chat.db on a named
# volume, set by docker-compose.yml. Setting it to a path that is not mounted
# means every redeploy wipes every account.
OMNIROUTE_DB_PATH=/data/chat.db

# Whether to believe x-forwarded-for when rate-limiting per client.
# TRUE when a reverse proxy or tunnel (Caddy, nginx, Cloudflare Tunnel) rewrites
# the header — which is the expected setup, since TLS is required.
# FALSE if the app is ever reachable directly: the header is client-controlled
# and a forged value would hand every request its own fresh rate-limit bucket.
#
# Get this wrong towards false and the per-IP limits share one bucket across all
# visitors (scaled up 25x to limit the damage). Get it wrong towards true and
# the limits are bypassable. The app warns at boot if it is unset.
OMNIROUTE_TRUST_PROXY=true

# How many proxies sit in front of the app. Only read when TRUST_PROXY is on.
#
# x-forwarded-for is a list that each proxy appends to, so the RIGHTMOST entry
# is the address your own proxy saw and the leftmost is whatever the client sent.
# The code counts this many entries in from the right (default 1).
#
#   1  Caddy/nginx/cloudflared talking straight to the container — the layout in
#      docker-compose.yml. Leave blank for this.
#   2  Cloudflare's proxy in front of a local terminator.
#
# Too small only means several visitors share a bucket. Too large means reading
# an entry the client controls, so the value is clamped and a client-supplied
# entry is never used as the key.
OMNIROUTE_PROXY_DEPTH=

# Escape hatch for the boot check. See the header. Leave unset.
OMNIROUTE_ALLOW_UNSAFE_CONFIG=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 11. Storage limits — how much history one account may keep
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Both are OFF by default, and off is the right default for a personal
# instance: nothing is ever deleted behind your back.
#
# Turn them on when the instance is open to other people, because the free-tier
# disk is the binding constraint and nothing else defends it. A 20 GB gp3 volume
# holding the OS, Docker images and the database has perhaps 12 GB spare; a
# handful of enthusiastic users with long conversations reach that in weeks, and
# the failure mode is not a polite error — SQLite cannot write, so sign-in,
# chat and payment reconciliation all stop at once.
#
# WHAT THIS IS NOT: it is not summarisation. Trimming is a DELETE. Summarising
# older turns would mean an LLM call on the write path of every message —
# spending someone's quota, able to hang, and impossible to roll back once the
# originals are gone. If conversation memory beyond the window is wanted, it
# belongs in a background job with its own table, not here.

# Keep at most this many chats per account; the oldest by last-activity are
# deleted (with their messages) when the count is exceeded. 0 or unset =
# unlimited.
OMNIROUTE_MAX_CHATS_PER_USER=

# Keep at most this many messages in one chat. The first 2 messages are always
# kept — they carry the framing the rest of the conversation refers back to —
# and the remainder of the budget goes to the most recent turns. Values below 4
# are raised to 4, because a smaller window cannot hold a coherent exchange.
# 0 or unset = unlimited. A trimmed chat records the number removed, and the
# transcript shows a one-line note above the first surviving message when it is
# reopened — so a conversation that starts mid-thread reads as a policy rather
# than as lost data.
OMNIROUTE_MAX_MESSAGES_PER_CHAT=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 12. VS Code bridge — how file tools reach a tester's own machine
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This section used to be titled "leave this alone", and said that the bridge
# authorises callers by one test — is the TCP peer 127.0.0.1 — and that the port
# must therefore never be published or proxied. Both statements are now out of
# date, and the old advice would have been actively wrong to follow, so here is
# what changed.
#
# The bridge no longer trusts the peer address, because behind a reverse proxy
# every caller on the internet arrives from 127.0.0.1 and would have passed. It
# now requires a pairing token on the WebSocket upgrade: the token is minted per
# account by /api/extension/token, stored only as a hash, and it resolves to one
# user id. An upgrade with no token, an unknown token or a revoked one is
# refused with 401 before any RPC frame is read. That token is the entire
# authorisation model — the network position of the caller is no longer part of
# it.
#
# What a connected socket then gets is NOT access to this server. Every file
# read, write and command is forwarded to that one user's editor and executed
# there. A production build has no fallback to this machine's filesystem: if the
# editor is not connected, the tool call fails. That is why file tools in §9 are
# now allowed in production at all.
#
# It still starts itself. src/lib/vscodeBridge.ts calls initialize() at import
# time on the server and the chat route imports that module, so the variable
# below is not inert documentation — it is what decides whether the listener
# binds. Leave it blank and no tester can connect an editor; the app still runs,
# chat still works, and the Connect VS Code panel says the operator has not
# enabled it.

# Set to the literal string "true" to accept editor connections. Any other
# value, including "TRUE" and "1", leaves the listener closed.
#
# Blank is the safe default and is correct if you are not offering the bridge.
# If you ARE offering it, know what leaving it blank looks like from the outside,
# because the two symptoms do not obviously share a cause:
#
#   - The web UI shows a red "Editor link off" pill.
#   - VS Code retries forever reporting that the server closed the connection.
#
# The second one misleads. Under docker-compose the port is published even when
# nothing binds it inside the container, so Docker's forwarder accepts the TCP
# connection and then resets it. The extension sees an established-then-dropped
# link rather than a refused one, which reads as a network fault. It is not a
# network fault; it is this variable.
OMNIROUTE_BRIDGE_ENABLE=

# TCP port for the bridge listener. Blank means 20129, which is what
# docker-compose.yml publishes and what the Caddyfile below forwards to. Change
# all three together or nothing connects.
OMNIROUTE_BRIDGE_PORT=

# The interface the listener binds. Blank means 127.0.0.1.
#
# docker-compose.yml already sets this to 0.0.0.0 and `environment:` beats this
# file, so under compose the value here is ignored — it is documented because
# the reason matters if you ever run outside compose. Loopback inside a
# container is the CONTAINER's loopback, so a published port would forward to
# nothing: the service reports healthy and every connection is refused. Binding
# 0.0.0.0 is safe here only because the port is published to the host's loopback
# and the token check is what actually authorises.
OMNIROUTE_BRIDGE_HOST=

# The address handed to the extension when it is paired. Set this.
#
# Left blank, the app guesses from the Host header of whichever request asked
# for a token. The guess is usually right and is wrong in exactly the cases that
# are hardest to debug — a tester behind a corporate proxy that rewrites Host,
# or a request that arrives without one. Being told beats guessing.
#
#   OMNIROUTE_BRIDGE_PUBLIC_URL=wss://omniroute-beta.duckdns.org/vscode-bridge
#
# wss://, not ws://. File contents travel over this socket in both directions;
# plain ws:// puts them in the clear across every network between the tester and
# this box. The boot check warns on both a missing value and a ws:// one.
#
# The path must match BRIDGE_PATH in src/lib/bridgeEndpoint.ts ("/vscode-bridge")
# and the route in the Caddyfile. A typo in any one of the three produces a
# connection that hangs rather than an error.
OMNIROUTE_BRIDGE_PUBLIC_URL=

# The Caddy configuration this expects — note that the bridge and the app are
# two different upstreams on one hostname, so `reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3005` on
# its own is not enough:
#
#   omniroute-beta.duckdns.org {
#     handle /vscode-bridge* { reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:20129 }
#     handle                 { reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3005  }
#   }
#
# Caddy proxies the WebSocket upgrade with no extra directives — until the
# moment the socket is handed over it is an ordinary HTTP request.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 13. Process limits
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Set in docker-compose.yml rather than here, and listed for reference because
# `environment:` in that file overrides anything written in this one.
#
#   NODE_OPTIONS=--max-old-space-size=896   V8 heap ceiling, in MiB.
#   mem_limit: 1600m                        container memory ceiling.
#
# They belong together. V8 sizes its default heap from /proc/meminfo, which
# inside a container reports the HOST's memory rather than the container's
# share — so without an explicit cap Node grows into memory the cgroup will not
# give it and the kernel kills the process. From the outside that looks like the
# app restarting at random under load.
#
# Both values are sized for a 2 GiB t4g.small. On a larger instance raise them
# together, keeping the heap cap near 60% of the container limit; the boot check
# prints the resolved pair on every start and warns if they drift apart.

